1
00:00:00,590 --> 00:00:02,532
Have you ever thought about windows?

2
00:00:02,894 --> 00:00:05,316
Like, really considered the idea?

3
00:00:05,742 --> 00:00:11,460
If we hadn't been exposed to them our entire lives, it might seem like kind of a bizarre concept.

4
00:00:11,918 --> 00:00:19,300
On the face of it, windows are about light, but it's more esoteric than that, especially now that we can make our own light.

5
00:00:19,680 --> 00:00:24,800
We want to be inside, but not all the way inside, so we put holes in the wall.

6
00:00:25,230 --> 00:00:26,308
It's a heck of a trade-off.

7
00:00:26,560 --> 00:00:30,276
You get light and a view of the outdoors, but it comes at a cost.

8
00:00:30,670 --> 00:00:41,828
Not just the cost of the glass itself, but also the structural changes they require and their effects on ventilation, sound, cooling, heating, safety, and security inside a building.

9
00:00:42,318 --> 00:00:54,692
As simple as they seem, windows are a technology of sorts, and like a lot of building science, we've slowly been making progress, readjusting those trade-offs and giving us the benefits without so many of the costs.

10
00:00:55,120 --> 00:01:01,280
I love to pick a relatively innocuous part of the built environment and really take a close look.

11
00:01:01,550 --> 00:01:07,120
So I built a few demonstrations in the garage to show you how far the science of glazing has come.

12
00:01:07,470 --> 00:01:09,668
I'm Grady, and this is Practical Engineering.

13
00:01:19,340 --> 00:01:24,452
Glass is not a good substitute for an exterior wall in many respects.

14
00:01:24,814 --> 00:01:29,820
If you take some wall away and replace it with a window, you're not trading like for like.

15
00:01:30,126 --> 00:01:33,764
For one, you never accidentally throw a baseball through a brick wall.

16
00:01:34,094 --> 00:01:36,516
But it's especially true when it comes to heat.

17
00:01:36,910 --> 00:01:47,716
And this is a pretty big deal because we collectively spend vast sums of money, resources, effort, and energy on maintaining a comfortable thermal climate inside our buildings.

18
00:01:48,240 --> 00:01:57,412
Almost all the time, in almost all the places, the temperature outside of a building is different from the temperature we want it to be inside.

19
00:01:57,902 --> 00:02:05,124
But the laws of physics, also all the time in all the places, are constantly working to equalize those temperatures.

20
00:02:05,582 --> 00:02:09,412
We usually categorize the flow of heat into three separate modes.

21
00:02:09,840 --> 00:02:19,908
Conduction is heat moving through solid objects, atoms or molecules that are tightly packed, transmitting energy to their neighbors directly by vibrating against each other.

22
00:02:20,160 --> 00:02:24,548
Convection is heat transfer via the movement of a fluid like air or water.

23
00:02:24,720 --> 00:02:28,196
Radiation is heat transfer through electromagnetic waves.

24
00:02:28,654 --> 00:02:32,260
Since it's literally light, radiation doesn't need a medium.

25
00:02:32,622 --> 00:02:37,668
All three play a role in the walls of buildings, and of course, it works both ways.

26
00:02:37,840 --> 00:02:44,164
Sometimes it's too hot outside and you want to keep that heat out, sometimes it's too cold and you want to keep the heat in.

27
00:02:44,590 --> 00:02:51,396
Any movement of heat from inside to out, or vice versa, is a flow of power, just like we measure with electricity.

28
00:02:51,758 --> 00:03:01,476
If you have 500 watts of heat flowing out of your house, you essentially need a 500-watt heater inside to make up for the loss and maintain a steady temperature.

29
00:03:01,760 --> 00:03:09,440
And just like a voltage is a difference in potential that drives electrical current, heat flow is driven by a difference in temperature.

30
00:03:09,774 --> 00:03:13,440
The bigger that difference, the greater the flow of heat through a given wall.

31
00:03:13,710 --> 00:03:23,360
Continuing the electrical metaphor, insulation is the generic term we use to describe materials that resist the transmission of heat from one side to the other.

32
00:03:23,630 --> 00:03:25,860
It makes sense that we put it in our walls.

33
00:03:26,254 --> 00:03:29,988
In the US, we use R value as a measure of this resistance.

34
00:03:30,542 --> 00:03:45,316
This is kind of a cursed unit of measurement, so I'm going to gloss over the scientific definition, but a typical exterior wall assembly will usually have an R value between 10 and 25, depending on climate and how new the building is.

35
00:03:45,678 --> 00:03:49,412
A simple pane of glass has an R value of about 1.

36
00:03:49,934 --> 00:03:50,916
Let me show that to you.

37
00:03:52,112 --> 00:03:55,392
I built this box of foam insulation in my garage.

38
00:03:55,598 --> 00:03:58,992
Inside, I have a 25 watt ceramic heat emitter.

39
00:03:59,312 --> 00:04:02,948
I can close this up and wait for the temperature inside to stabilize.

40
00:04:03,246 --> 00:04:09,060
When that happens, we can assume that the heat going into the box is the same as the heat flowing out.

41
00:04:09,518 --> 00:04:11,632
Okay, this is pretty much settled out.

42
00:04:11,712 --> 00:04:18,672
You can see the difference between the interior and the exterior probes is about 43 degrees Fahrenheit.

43
00:04:18,958 --> 00:04:20,964
I think that's around 24 Celsius.

44
00:04:21,582 --> 00:04:23,472
This is R5 foam.

45
00:04:23,758 --> 00:04:32,676
So if you do the math for a perfectly sealed box with these dimensions, that should be right around 50 degrees Fahrenheit, 28 Celsius.

46
00:04:33,072 --> 00:04:38,468
Um so accounting for the imperfections in my setup, like this terrible tape seal.

47
00:04:38,798 --> 00:04:40,132
That's actually pretty close.

48
00:04:40,366 --> 00:04:44,992
Um, so now I'm gonna swap this lid out with a pane of glass and we'll set this going again.

49
00:04:45,472 --> 00:04:55,952
Okay, this is settled out, and you can see that the temperature inside the shop hasn't really changed, but the temperature inside the box has gone down a lot compared to the foam lid.

50
00:04:56,046 --> 00:05:02,148
The difference is about 22 degrees Fahrenheit, which I think is around 13 Celsius.

51
00:05:02,542 --> 00:05:13,924
So just replacing one side of the box with glass instead of foam, it takes a much smaller difference in temperature to move that same 25 watts out of the box.

52
00:05:14,382 --> 00:05:15,952
I'm sure you see the issue here.

53
00:05:16,112 --> 00:05:20,836
If we want windows in our buildings, it comes at a cost of thermal efficiency.

54
00:05:21,166 --> 00:05:36,672
For every square foot or square meter of exterior wall that we swap with single-pane windows, heat will move through that space roughly 10 to 25 times faster, so we rarely use single-pane glazing anymore.

55
00:05:36,942 --> 00:05:44,068
Almost all modern windows use two and sometimes even three panes of glass with a sealed air gap between them.

56
00:05:44,366 --> 00:05:51,172
Sometimes this gap is filled with a heavy gas like argon or krypton that has lower conduction and convection.

57
00:05:51,534 --> 00:05:57,792
There are even vacuum-insulated panels that have no air inside, eliminating convection entirely.

58
00:05:58,126 --> 00:06:01,252
It seems like a simple change, but it makes a pretty big difference.

59
00:06:01,678 --> 00:06:06,180
I happen to have a double pane glass unit, so we can try the experiment again.

60
00:06:06,638 --> 00:06:14,352
Okay, this demo settled down now, and you can see that the temperature difference is right at 30 degrees F17C.

61
00:06:14,830 --> 00:06:23,312
Remember with the foam lid it was about 43 degrees, and with the single pane it was around 23 degrees, so this is basically right in the middle.

62
00:06:23,392 --> 00:06:28,420
It's not as good as when there was no window, but it's a lot better than the single pane of glass.

63
00:06:28,782 --> 00:06:43,492
This varies a lot with details of the frame and coatings, but very generally, modern double pane windows can double to quadruple the insulated value of a single pane of glass, and that can make a huge difference in a building with a lot of them.

64
00:06:43,886 --> 00:06:48,192
This idea of a sealed air gap between the panes comes with one problem.

65
00:06:48,462 --> 00:06:52,192
If you've ever brought a bag of chips on an airplane, you know what I'm talking about.

66
00:06:52,430 --> 00:07:04,292
If the glass manufacturer is at a low elevation and the window is going to be installed somewhere much higher, you end up with an overpressure situation between the panes compared to the barometric pressure.

67
00:07:04,590 --> 00:07:07,108
That can cause them to bow out and even fracture.

68
00:07:07,598 --> 00:07:13,764
In some cases, manufacturers will actually dial in the pressure for the altitude of the final destination.

69
00:07:14,190 --> 00:07:19,940
Another option is to install a capillary tube that equalizes the pressure inside and out.

70
00:07:20,272 --> 00:07:30,948
These are very narrow diameter and long tubes meant to reduce the amount of moisture that diffuses between the panes, which would otherwise lead to condensation inside the unit.

71
00:07:31,406 --> 00:07:39,332
Insulating glass panels helps reduce heat conduction and convection, but that's not the only way heat gets into and out of a building.

72
00:07:39,552 --> 00:07:46,980
The whole point of a window is to let in light, and we already learned that heat can move as electromagnetic waves.

73
00:07:47,470 --> 00:07:53,124
Of course, if we block visible light through a window to avoid heat transfer, that kind of defeats the purpose.

74
00:07:53,646 --> 00:07:58,500
But radiation also happens at other wavelengths on the electromagnetic spectrum.

75
00:07:58,990 --> 00:08:09,956
Specifically, for the temperatures we deal with on a day-to-day basis, most of the thermal radiation happens in infrared, wavelengths longer than visible light, but shorter than microwaves.

76
00:08:10,446 --> 00:08:15,552
Even within that range, there are big differences in how the waves interact with matter.

77
00:08:15,822 --> 00:08:18,148
That's particularly true for windows.

78
00:08:18,510 --> 00:08:27,652
Typical stuff at ambient temperatures like a sun-baked driveway or a human body emit thermal radiation mostly in the far infrared spectrum.

79
00:08:27,982 --> 00:08:31,044
This is the stuff you can see on a normal infrared camera.

80
00:08:31,278 --> 00:08:39,172
And even though glass is designed to let visible light through, it is almost completely opaque to long wave infrared light.

81
00:08:39,502 --> 00:08:45,348
Try looking through a window with an infrared camera, and you just see the window itself, not anything beyond.

82
00:08:46,062 --> 00:08:56,356
It's cool to think that if our eyes worked a bit differently, if they were tuned to a slightly different part of the electromagnetic spectrum, a glass window would be entirely useless.

83
00:08:56,750 --> 00:09:02,240
And it seems like it would be a good thing for our thermal problem, preventing heat from transferring through.

84
00:09:02,542 --> 00:09:04,420
Unfortunately, it's not that simple.

85
00:09:04,782 --> 00:09:09,668
Glass isn't just opaque to far IR, it's also highly emissive.

86
00:09:09,870 --> 00:09:14,500
That means it mostly absorbs thermal radiation rather than reflecting it.

87
00:09:14,862 --> 00:09:25,520
So the radiation doesn't come directly through, but it does warm the glass, which can then conduct through the pane and re-radiate it where you don't want it to go, whether inside or out.

88
00:09:25,680 --> 00:09:28,324
It's basically radiation with extra steps.

89
00:09:28,750 --> 00:09:33,700
On the shorter wavelength part of the spectrum we call near IR, it's a little different.

90
00:09:34,126 --> 00:09:42,720
The main source of near IR radiation is the sun, and like visible light, that radiation can pass directly through most glass.

91
00:09:42,990 --> 00:09:44,640
This is how greenhouses work.

92
00:09:44,814 --> 00:09:54,400
The shortwave radiation passes right through, where it can warm the stuff inside, but the stuff inside radiates long wave IR, which can't pass back out of the glass.

93
00:09:54,734 --> 00:10:09,668
So windows are kind of a two way half door for far infrared, since they absorb and re-radiate it in both directions, and a one-way door for near infrared, but we can do better than that, mostly through low emissivity or low E coatings.

94
00:10:10,158 --> 00:10:18,372
These coatings essentially convert a pane of glass into a mirror for IR, and you can see the difference really well with an IR camera.

95
00:10:18,830 --> 00:10:26,560
You still get some reflections with an uncoated pane, but the difference is obvious when you put a coated pane next to an uncoated one.

96
00:10:26,798 --> 00:10:30,628
But this gets a little tricky, especially when it comes to near IR.

97
00:10:30,926 --> 00:10:38,596
If you live in a cold climate and your main use of energy is heating, you're happy to invite as much sunlight as you can get inside.

98
00:10:38,862 --> 00:10:41,572
You don't want to reflect the near IR.

99
00:10:41,840 --> 00:10:43,844
The opposite is true in warm climates.

100
00:10:44,430 --> 00:10:48,452
Fortunately, manufacturers can tune the coatings according to the climate.

101
00:10:48,654 --> 00:10:56,000
You can reflect shortwave radiation if you're worried most about cooling, or you can let it in to gather more heat inside.

102
00:10:56,366 --> 00:10:59,920
Window manufacturers also sometimes vary which face is coated.

103
00:11:00,334 --> 00:11:10,500
In warmer climates, you can coat the inside of the outer pane, called surface 2, reflecting the IR before it crosses the gap to keep it from warming the air between the panes.

104
00:11:10,830 --> 00:11:18,692
In cooler climates, you can put it on surface 3 to reflect long wave IR back into your house without it having to cross the air gap.

105
00:11:19,214 --> 00:11:23,908
Making windows more thermally efficient isn't the only use for glass coatings.

106
00:11:24,334 --> 00:11:28,452
If you're like me, there's a good chance you have the Merlin app installed on your phone.

107
00:11:28,718 --> 00:11:34,564
I think it's a bit of an inevitability that once you reach a certain age, you start getting interested in birds.

108
00:11:35,054 --> 00:11:41,860
Unfortunately, one of the largest human-caused sources of bird mortality is actually windows.

109
00:11:42,126 --> 00:11:50,916
At certain angles, windows work essentially like mirrors, reflecting the trees and sky, and birds, bless their hearts, can't always tell the difference.

110
00:11:51,342 --> 00:11:52,640
It can happen the other way too.

111
00:11:52,800 --> 00:12:01,828
Sometimes a bird will see something inside a building or all the way through two windows that they want to fly toward, not recognizing that there's solid glass in the way.

112
00:12:02,062 --> 00:12:11,440
For you and me, walking into a glass pane like a sliding door is embarrassing, but for a bird flying at much higher speeds with tiny hollow bones, it's often fatal.

113
00:12:11,886 --> 00:12:16,960
McCormick Place Convention Center on the Lakeshore in Chicago may be the most famous case study.

114
00:12:17,166 --> 00:12:23,652
In an average year, monitors would find around 1,000 dead birds around the building from collisions with the windows.

115
00:12:23,950 --> 00:12:28,612
In October 2023, nearly a thousand birds died in a single night.

116
00:12:28,974 --> 00:12:36,000
The owner finally decided to address the problem head-on, installing a pattern of dots on all the glass surrounding the building.

117
00:12:36,302 --> 00:12:39,876
The solution cut the number of bird strikes by 95%.

118
00:12:40,750 --> 00:12:45,988
Awareness about birdsafe glass is continuing to grow, and so are the number of solutions.

119
00:12:46,350 --> 00:12:50,400
Many glass manufacturers have birdsafe products that work in a similar way.

120
00:12:50,670 --> 00:13:00,100
The goal is to break the illusion of glass being a mirror or imperceptible as a solid surface, so most of the fixes involve patterns or designs on the glass.

121
00:13:00,494 --> 00:13:14,692
I think it looks pretty cool actually, and because many bird species can see into the ultraviolet spectrum, you can even get dots and patterns that are practically invisible to humans, but still perceptible to birds to keep them from crashing into windows.

122
00:13:15,214 --> 00:13:26,980
Some cities are already including requirements for bird-safe glass into codes, particularly in major migration corridors and for buildings that use large, uninterrupted facades of glass.

123
00:13:27,278 --> 00:13:30,672
This is a really common design for tall buildings and skyscrapers.

124
00:13:30,832 --> 00:13:34,752
Ever since I was a kid, I've always been impressed with this kind of facade.

125
00:13:34,958 --> 00:13:41,700
From the outside, it looks like a sleek, continuous column of glass, and it's pretty interesting how it's actually done.

126
00:13:42,062 --> 00:13:50,660
These are called walls, specifically curtain walls, but they're also not walls in the traditional sense, at least when it comes to bearing loads.

127
00:13:51,118 --> 00:13:59,232
We typically think of a building's exterior walls as the primary load path, the way forces from the building are transferred into the foundation.

128
00:13:59,502 --> 00:14:01,312
With curtain walls, that's not true.

129
00:14:01,518 --> 00:14:03,392
And that name kind of gives it away.

130
00:14:03,662 --> 00:14:07,876
In buildings with curtain walls, the load bearing happens through interior columns.

131
00:14:08,142 --> 00:14:10,180
The exterior walls just hang there.

132
00:14:10,510 --> 00:14:15,268
Not that it needs another analogy, but it's pretty much like the building is wearing a coat.

133
00:14:15,632 --> 00:14:17,312
This has a lot of advantages.

134
00:14:17,472 --> 00:14:21,860
A curtain wall is relatively lightweight, reducing structural loads on a building.

135
00:14:22,126 --> 00:14:26,564
It's also thin, maximizing space in a building where it comes at a premium.

136
00:14:26,862 --> 00:14:28,388
They're also unitized.

137
00:14:28,686 --> 00:14:34,112
You get very large pieces assembled in a factory so they go up quickly on a construction site.

138
00:14:34,382 --> 00:14:39,780
And of course, you get a lot of light into the building when the entire exterior wall is a window.

139
00:14:40,174 --> 00:14:42,116
But how do you hold the glass in place?

140
00:14:42,670 --> 00:14:48,484
The process of installing glass, called glazing, is not like installing most other materials.

141
00:14:48,846 --> 00:14:51,876
You can't use traditional fasteners like nails or screws.

142
00:14:52,270 --> 00:14:57,060
Glass is brittle, and the sharp point forces would induce too much stress.

143
00:14:57,390 --> 00:15:12,676
If you look at the windows of your house, the glass panes are usually sandwiched inside a frame with softer material that both seals against the glass to keep out the weather and spreads out the holding forces across a larger area along the edges of the glass.

144
00:15:13,134 --> 00:15:15,552
That's sometimes called captured glazing.

145
00:15:15,790 --> 00:15:21,072
It's done on large buildings with expansive glass facades too, but it's not the only way.

146
00:15:21,326 --> 00:15:36,900
When you really want the outside of a building to look like only glass with no mullions or framing elements protruding beyond the panes, you can use structural silicon glazing, where the sealant does some or all the work to hold the glass in place.

147
00:15:37,262 --> 00:15:43,076
In four-sided structural glazing, the glass panels are adhesively bonded on all four edges.

148
00:15:43,312 --> 00:15:46,820
You have nothing on the exterior except smooth glass.

149
00:15:47,150 --> 00:15:53,792
If you're used to the silicone sealant sold at the hardware store, this might seem absolutely crazy to you.

150
00:15:54,062 --> 00:16:03,556
I definitely wouldn't trust a tube of bathroom caulk to hold thousands of pounds of glass hundreds of feet into the air above sidewalks and roads full of people.

151
00:16:03,982 --> 00:16:18,820
To be fair, these systems do usually have some kind of mechanical clips or toggles that surreptitiously prevent panes from coming entirely loose if the sealant fails, but also, structural silicone is very different from caulk.

152
00:16:19,232 --> 00:16:22,020
Just to demonstrate this, I have a glass block.

153
00:16:22,318 --> 00:16:28,004
First, I'm going to attach this to a metal plate using typical silicone caulk you might use in the bathroom.

154
00:16:28,686 --> 00:16:33,060
I'm starting with a 45 pound weight on the hook, which is probably a bit ambitious.

155
00:16:33,870 --> 00:16:40,420
And of course the camera died right when that dropped, but it held 45 pounds for just about a minute before it dropped.

156
00:16:40,654 --> 00:16:48,516
And part of that's because even after two weeks of curing, you can see only the caulk around the edges actually cured.

157
00:16:48,910 --> 00:16:51,620
This stuff's just not designed for heavy weights like this.

158
00:16:52,174 --> 00:16:54,756
Now I'll try it with structural silicone.

159
00:16:54,992 --> 00:17:01,860
This stuff comes from a few manufacturers in a variety of strengths, curing systems, and material compatibilities.

160
00:17:02,190 --> 00:17:10,084
After I let this cure for a week, which is when the manufacturer says it reaches 50% of its strength, I tried my little demo again.

161
00:17:10,512 --> 00:17:13,732
Wow, it's holding 45 pounds just fine.

162
00:17:14,862 --> 00:17:19,620
So I added the small metal anvil just for good measure, and it's still holding fine.

163
00:17:20,398 --> 00:17:29,376
Just a silly little experiment like this can give you a lot more confidence that those big insulated glass units on tall buildings aren't going anywhere.

164
00:17:29,710 --> 00:17:33,616
But the engineering of windows doesn't stop at holding them in place.

165
00:17:33,856 --> 00:17:37,216
We also have to think about what happens if they break.

166
00:17:37,582 --> 00:17:43,616
The most typical glass we think about with picture frames and windows is actually annealed glass.

167
00:17:43,886 --> 00:17:46,656
The manufacturing process is pretty impressive.

168
00:17:46,958 --> 00:17:56,356
The molten material is allowed to spread out and float atop a bed of molten tin, making it extremely flat and very consistent in thickness.

169
00:17:56,750 --> 00:18:01,380
Then the glass is allowed to cool slowly, which relieves internal stresses.

170
00:18:01,678 --> 00:18:06,372
That makes it possible to cut along clean lines and even drill holes in the material.

171
00:18:06,702 --> 00:18:15,812
But it also means that when annealed glass breaks, it usually fractures into large, razor-sharp shards that can be extremely dangerous.

172
00:18:16,558 --> 00:18:28,176
I think most people have enough experience with the issue that there's hardly a need at all to say that broken glass is a serious hazard, and in many cases, it's a simply unacceptable hazard.

173
00:18:28,430 --> 00:18:35,136
A slip in a shower that breaks the door, a toddler on a glass tabletop, even pushing too hard on a glass door.

174
00:18:35,406 --> 00:18:46,020
There are plenty of documented cases where a simple accident became fatal simply because of how annealed glass becomes jagged and razor sharp when it fractures.

175
00:18:46,382 --> 00:18:50,256
One of the main solutions to the problem is tempering the glass.

176
00:18:50,574 --> 00:19:00,196
If you heat glass and cool it down quickly, often using high-pressure blasts of air, you can get the outside to cool a little quicker than the inside of the glass.

177
00:19:00,462 --> 00:19:08,676
The outside faces shrink, then when the inside cools, it shrinks as well, pulling the outside faces of the glass into compression.

178
00:19:09,102 --> 00:19:14,244
If you use a polarizing filter, sometimes you can even see the stresses in tempered glass.

179
00:19:14,734 --> 00:19:20,736
This is a tricky process because if the cooling happens unevenly, the glass will crack or break.

180
00:19:21,038 --> 00:19:31,940
I've discovered this myself by accidentally setting a glass dish on the stove burner, but if you do it right, tempering glass makes it much stronger than typical annealed glass.

181
00:19:32,334 --> 00:19:36,964
Since the outer faces are in compression, cracks are less able to propagate.

182
00:19:37,230 --> 00:19:41,412
But even if it does break, tempered glass has a big advantage.

183
00:19:42,286 --> 00:19:46,820
Instead of the large shards, you get tiny, relatively harmless pieces.

184
00:19:47,182 --> 00:19:53,696
These can still have sharp edges, but just being so much smaller means they're much less able to injure someone.

185
00:19:53,934 --> 00:20:02,372
They have a lot less mass when they fall, and the edges are shorter, so it's more like angry gravel and less like deadly knives and spears.

186
00:20:02,798 --> 00:20:17,028
That's why tempered glass is often called safety glass, and lots of building codes require it in situations where broken glass can pose a safety hazard like glass doors, large windows close to the ground, and in bathrooms and showers.

187
00:20:17,454 --> 00:20:23,428
Most vehicles also use tempered glass on the side windows to reduce the risk of injury during a crash.

188
00:20:23,790 --> 00:20:28,772
One disadvantage of tempered glass is that it can't be modified after the fact.

189
00:20:29,070 --> 00:20:37,028
All that built-up stress in the material makes sure it shatters into tiny pieces, but it also prevents you from cutting or drilling holes.

190
00:20:37,486 --> 00:20:39,716
Tempering has to be the final step.

191
00:20:40,238 --> 00:20:44,016
The other disadvantage also comes from its main advantage.

192
00:20:44,334 --> 00:20:50,416
Because tempered glass breaks in a relatively safe way, it doesn't offer much security.

193
00:20:50,638 --> 00:20:53,124
And really it's true for annealed glass too.

194
00:20:53,390 --> 00:20:59,076
Windows aren't just a thermal weak point in buildings, they are also a security weak point.

195
00:20:59,406 --> 00:21:04,996
Why would a criminal struggle to break through a reinforced door if they can just quickly smash a window?

196
00:21:05,216 --> 00:21:07,780
That's where laminated glass comes in.

197
00:21:08,142 --> 00:21:22,496
You can glue individual panes of glass together where they still retain almost perfect optical clarity, but even better than that, you can sandwich a tough plastic inner layer between those panes of glass, creating a composite material.

198
00:21:22,766 --> 00:21:25,060
And it behaves a lot differently when it breaks.

199
00:21:25,614 --> 00:21:28,164
Now you don't have shards separating at all.

200
00:21:28,462 --> 00:21:34,020
Even completely cracked apart, the glass stays adhered to the plastic instead of flying apart.

201
00:21:34,350 --> 00:21:38,340
And more importantly, this is still a barrier even after it breaks.

202
00:21:38,734 --> 00:21:40,496
I'm not getting my hand through here.

203
00:21:41,006 --> 00:21:56,228
Laminated glass is ideal for overhead applications where a break could otherwise rain down shards on people below, and it's perfect for commercial buildings, especially on ground floors, to make it more challenging for someone to break in through a window or glass door.

204
00:21:56,526 --> 00:22:06,576
Laminated glass is also standard on vehicle windshields to offer more protection from a road hazard that could otherwise break through the glass and injure the driver or passengers.

205
00:22:06,862 --> 00:22:18,564
What's kind of crazy is that I'm just scratching the surface of modern window technology, not to mention the glass itself, which can get a lot more complicated for applications like phone screens.

206
00:22:19,056 --> 00:22:27,972
But these are all innovations that are kind of hiding in plain sight, and to me at least, they make the view through a window just a little bit sweeter.

207
